{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## The defect\n\n`test.yml`'s `concurrency` group makes the 6-hourly SCHEDULED run and any manual\n`workflow_dispatch` on `main` mutually cancelling. Whichever starts second kills the first. Because\nmain's health signal is **only** the scheduled run — `main` is deliberately excluded from the push\ntrigger — a cancelled scheduled run means **no main-health reading until the next 6-hour boundary**.\n\nThe workflow's own comment states the intent: the branch-health signal is \"is main healthy NOW\",\nnot \"was every commit green\". A silent cancellation defeats exactly that.\n\n## It happened TWICE in one day\n\n```\n33874909241  main 69cd2b9e1  schedule           CANCELLED  12:51:27\n33875177644  main 1af450654  workflow_dispatch  success    12:54:34   <- cancelled the 12:51 schedule\n\n33905162633  main ee2c54f88  workflow_dispatch  CANCELLED  18:18:15   <- killed by the 18:45 schedule\n33907581465  main cfff861c0  schedule           running    18:45:19\n```\n\nThe first cost a scheduled reading entirely. The second cost a manual reading that had already\nproduced five confirmed job failures and was cancelled mid-flight, taking `sweep (macos, full\nsuite)` down with it before it could report.\n\n## Why it is worth fixing rather than just knowing\n\nThe cancellation is SILENT in the only place anyone looks. `gh run list` shows `cancelled`, which\nreads as \"someone stopped it\", not as \"main's health is now unknown for up to six hours\". An\noperator or agent asking \"is main green?\" sees a cancelled run and has no signal that the answer is\nunavailable rather than merely interrupted.\n\nIt also creates a perverse incentive: an agent wanting an on-demand reading near a 6-hour boundary\nwill destroy the scheduled reading to get it, or lose its own — and cannot tell in advance which,\nbecause the cron carries jitter (nominal minute 41; the 18:45 run fired four minutes late).\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [x] AC1 — A manual `workflow_dispatch` of `test.yml` on `main` and the scheduled run no longer\n      cancel one another. Whatever the mechanism (a concurrency group keyed to include the event or\n      the sha, `cancel-in-progress: false` for this lane, or refusing a redundant dispatch), BOTH\n      readings must be obtainable.\n- [x] AC2 — Redundant work is still bounded. This must not become \"every dispatch queues another\n      full sweep\", which is the cost the concurrency group exists to control. State explicitly what\n      the new bound is.\n- [x] AC3 — When a main-health run IS cancelled or superseded for any reason, that fact is\n      distinguishable from \"main is unhealthy\" and from \"main is healthy\" — a reader must be able to\n      tell that the signal is UNAVAILABLE. Absence of a reading must not present as either outcome.\n- [x] AC4 [non-codebase] — Reproduce the collision (dispatch near a schedule boundary) and demonstrate both runs\n      surviving, or the dispatch being refused with a clear reason. A fix not exercised against the\n      real collision has not been shown to work.\n\n## Non-goals\n\nNot re-enabling the push trigger on `main` — it is excluded for a documented and good reason (on\n`main`, `github.ref` is the constant `refs/heads/main`, so every push cancelled its predecessor and\nGitHub rendered a healthy main as a trail of red X's). Not changing what the sweep lanes test.\n\n## Completion evidence\n\nGerrit 2950 changed `test.yml` from `test-${{ github.ref }}` to\n`test-${{ github.event_name }}-${{ github.ref }}`. The regression test renders the\nworkflow expression for `schedule` and `workflow_dispatch` on `refs/heads/main` and\nproves those runs no longer share a cancellation key (AC1). Redundant work remains\nbounded to one in-flight run per event/ref pair because `cancel-in-progress: true`\nremains enabled on the event-qualified group (AC2). `main_health_report.py` now\nclassifies empty, cancelled, skipped, and other incomplete all-non-failing gate sets\nas `UNAVAILABLE`, while any real failed gate still renders RED (AC3).\n\nAC4 is tagged non-codebase because forcing GitHub cron jitter near a six-hour schedule\nboundary is not a deterministic local code exercise. The merged workflow expression\nis the GitHub-owned mechanism that decides cancellation groups; the policy test\nevaluates that expression directly for the two real event contexts, and Gerrit 2950\nthen passed the real GitHub workflow on patch set 2."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1789017249684975001,"uuid":"dcd4e66a-f23c-47e8-bac7-cde90e8e1b3f"}